<article class="env-example" id="04JC">
<p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/04JC">Example <span data-tag="04JC">6.2.2.15</span>.</a> Let $\operatorname{Top}$ denote the category whose objects are topological spaces and whose morphisms are continuous functions. Let us regard $\operatorname{Top}$ as a simplicial category (Example <a data-tag="00JV" href="/tag/00JV">2.4.1.5</a>), and let $\operatorname{\mathcal{T}}= \operatorname{N}_{\bullet }^{\operatorname{hc}}( \operatorname{Top})$ denote its homotopy coherent nerve. Let $\operatorname{\mathcal{T}}_{0} \subseteq \operatorname{\mathcal{T}}$ be the full subcategory spanned by those topological spaces which have the homotopy type of a CW complex. Then: </p>
<p><ul>
<li><p>
A continuous function between topological spaces $f: X \rightarrow Y$ exhibits $X$ as a $\operatorname{\mathcal{T}}_0$-coreflection of $Y$ if and only if $X$ has the homotopy type of a CW complex and $f$ is a weak homotopy equivalence. This is restatement of Corollary <a data-tag="04HV" href="/tag/04HV">3.6.5.4</a>. 
</p>
</li><li><p>
The full subcategory $\operatorname{\mathcal{T}}_{0} \subseteq \operatorname{\mathcal{T}}$ is coreflective. That is, for every topological space $Y$, there exists a weak homotopy equivalence $f: X \rightarrow Y$, where $X$ has the homotopy type of a CW complex. For example, we can take $f$ to be the counit map $| \operatorname{Sing}_{\bullet }(Y) | \rightarrow Y$ (see Corollary <a data-tag="0143" href="/tag/0143">3.6.4.2</a>). 
</p>
</li>
</ul></p>
</article>
